&lt;p&gt;This transformation is orthogonal, which means that the inverse transformation is simply the transpose of the matrix. This property ensures that distances and angles are preserved — a crucial feature of rigid rotations. The inverse transformation, which is needed to express the rotated parabola in terms of the original coordinates, is:&lt;/p&gt;
